## LAB 26: Text Utilities

### Setup
```bash
cat > ~/textutils_data.txt << 'EOF'
John,Engineering,75000
Sarah,Marketing,65000
Mike,Engineering,72000
Lisa,Sales,60000
John,Engineering,75000
EOF
```
```bash
cat ~/textutils_data.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 1:** shows the sample data file contents.

### cut — single column
```bash
cut -d',' -f1 ~/textutils_data.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 2:** shows just the names column.

### cut — multiple columns
```bash
cut -d',' -f1,3 ~/textutils_data.txt
```

### sort — default
```bash
sort ~/textutils_data.txt
```

### sort — by column, numeric
```bash
sort -t',' -k3 -n ~/textutils_data.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 3:** shows salaries sorted lowest to highest.

### sort — reverse
```bash
sort -t',' -k3 -nr ~/textutils_data.txt
```

### uniq — remove duplicates
```bash
sort ~/textutils_data.txt | uniq
```

### uniq -c — count duplicates
```bash
sort ~/textutils_data.txt | uniq -c
```
📸 **Screenshot 4:** shows counts, with `John,Engineering,75000` showing count `2`.

### tr — uppercase transform
```bash
tr 'a-z' 'A-Z' < ~/textutils_data.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 5:** shows all-uppercase output.

### tr -d — delete characters
```bash
tr -d ',' < ~/textutils_data.txt
```

### wc — full count
```bash
wc ~/textutils_data.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 6:** shows lines/words/characters count (e.g. `5 15 95 ...`).

### wc -l — lines only
```bash
wc -l ~/textutils_data.txt
```

### Bonus: a pipeline combining utilities (for your "pipeline" screenshot)
```bash
cut -d',' -f2 ~/textutils_data.txt | sort | uniq -c
```
📸 **Screenshot 7:** shows department names counted (this satisfies the "pipeline combining utilities" evidence item).

