## LAB 27: Regular Expressions

### Setup
```bash
cat > ~/regex_test.txt << 'EOF'
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog
Email: john@example.com
Phone: 555-1234
Date: 2026-07-18
Price: $99.99
Username: user123
Password: Pass@Word123
EOF
```
```bash
cat ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 1:** shows the sample text file.

### Literal string match
```bash
grep "fox" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 2:** shows the fox line.

### Dot (.) metacharacter
```bash
grep "d.g" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 3:** shows the dot metacharacter matching "dog".

### Asterisk (*) and Plus (+) quantifiers
```bash
grep "fo*x" ~/regex_test.txt
grep -E "fo+x" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 4:** capture one or both of these (quantifiers).

### Character classes
```bash
grep "[0-9]" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 5:** shows all lines containing digits.

### Negated character class
```bash
grep "[^0-9]" ~/regex_test.txt
```

### Start-of-line anchor
```bash
grep "^Email" ~/regex_test.txt
```

### End-of-line anchor
```bash
grep "dog$" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 6:** capture either or both anchor commands together.

### Email pattern match
```bash
grep -E "[a-zA-Z0-9]+@[a-zA-Z0-9]+\.[a-zA-Z]+" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 7:** shows the matched email line.

### Phone number pattern match
```bash
grep -E "[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}" ~/regex_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 8:** shows the matched phone line.

### Case-insensitive match
```bash
grep -i "password" ~/regex_test.txt
```

