## LAB 28: The Vi Editor

### Setup (run this in the terminal, NOT inside vi)
```bash
cat > ~/vi_test.txt << 'EOF'
This is line one
This is line two
This is line three
This is line four
This is line five
EOF
```

### Open the file in vi
```bash
vi ~/vi_test.txt
```
📸 **Screenshot 1:** take this right after opening — shows the file content, the `~` on any empty lines, and the filename/status line at the bottom.

### Navigate (these are keystrokes, typed with NO Enter key, while in command mode — don't press `i` first)
Type these one at a time and watch the cursor move:
- `j` (down), `k` (up), `h` (left), `l` (right)
- `w` (next word), `b` (previous word)
- `0` (start of line), `$` (end of line)
- `G` (end of file), then `1G` (back to start)

📸 **Screenshot 2:** take this while your cursor is somewhere in the middle of the file after moving around, to show navigation.

### Enter insert mode and type text
Press:
```
i
```
You'll see `-- INSERT --` at the bottom. Now type some new text, e.g.:
```
This is a new line I am adding.
```
📸 **Screenshot 3:** capture while `-- INSERT --` is showing and your new text is visible.

### Exit insert mode
Press:
```
Esc
```

### Delete text (in command mode)
Position your cursor on a word, then try:
```
dw
```
Then try deleting a whole line:
```
dd
```
📸 **Screenshot 4:** take this immediately after `dd`, showing a line has disappeared.

### Undo
Press:
```
u
```
📸 (optional) capture this showing the deleted line restored.

### Redo
Press:
```
Ctrl+r
```

### Search for text
Type:
```
/fox
```
then press Enter. (Note: your practice file doesn't contain "fox" — for this step, either search for a word that IS in your file, e.g. `/line`, or add the word "fox" into the file first with `i`, `Esc`.)
📸 **Screenshot 5:** shows the cursor jumped to the matched word, highlighted.

Press `n` to go to the next match.

### Search and replace
Type exactly:
```
:%s/line/LINE/g
```
then press Enter.
📸 **Screenshot 6:** shows every "line" replaced with "LINE" in the file.

### Save the file
Type:
```
:w
```
then press Enter.
📸 **Screenshot 7:** shows the "written" confirmation message at the bottom of the screen.

### Save and quit
Type:
```
:wq
```
then press Enter.
📸 **Screenshot 8:** take this right after — shows you're back at the normal shell prompt.

### (For documentation only — do this separately, don't do it right after saving real work)
To practice quitting without saving:
```bash
vi ~/vi_test.txt
```
Make a small edit with `i`, type something, press `Esc`, then type:
```
:q!
```
and press Enter. This discards the edit and exits.

